        "at": "2026-10-20T12:07:32.377535+00:00",
        "symbol": "SP500",
        "action": "hold",
        "summary": "Book flat at GBP 1,500 with zero positions; no directional SP500 thesis has matured, so hold stands. Fresh quote (bid 7672.3 / ask 7672.8, quote_time 1792498011) has drifted below the prior decision's 7690.6/7691.1 and sits below both the 20-session SMA (7736.94) and 50-session SMA (7705.23), with RSI 48.2 and a negative MACD histogram (-11.24) — a mild continuation of the rate-capped grind, resolving only a pricing objection. The Oct 20 ONS labour release has now landed: BBC reporting (unverified single-publisher, c1fb64abaf970055, 6406ed19b596a94d) describes a falling headline jobless rate but falling vacancies as small firms cut hiring citing labour costs. If corroborated, this softens the UK second-round wage case the BoE dissents lean on, narrowing — not yet resolving — the global-tightening branch. The Oct 21 CPI print remains the decisive scheduled test: an energy-only rise with soft labour weakens the escalation branch per the standing invalidation. The verified policy backdrop is unchanged: Fed 12-0 hike to 3.75-4.00% with minutes text confirming elevated inflation and 'will deliver price stability' (c4766cd2c8ccfd44, e4ca17945c0d6c56); BoE 6-3 hold with three hike votes, CPI 3.1% likely rising, upside risks (6c6b04263fe1e72b, 119ab1d3fbfe56ef, 3257cb6a1f95a005); Brent $126.55 sustains the energy channel (f0649cf2ac4fbbe7). The ~89bp 2y-over-funds gap stays undecomposed, and the credit question remains one correlated cluster pending multi-session breadth. Initiating today would express one unresolved shared factor hours before its scheduled CPI test; long financing remains ~-0.16 GBP per 0.1 lot daily, metadata not measured economics.",
        "thesis": "Hold on SP500 is a portfolio-level decision, unchanged across four prior approved holds. Verified standing facts: Fed target 3.75-4.00% from a 12-0 hike (c4766cd2c8ccfd44), corroborated by readable FOMC minutes text noting inflation elevated, geopolitical energy pressure and the 'will deliver price stability' commitment (e4ca17945c0d6c56); BoE Bank Rate 3.75% (119ab1d3fbfe56ef) held 6-3 with three hike dissents, CPI 3.1% in August likely rising further, and inflation risks judged tilted upside (6c6b04263fe1e72b, 3257cb6a1f95a005); UK unemployment 4.9% May-Jul (f86574b608ca7c64); Brent $126.55 sustaining the energy-inflation channel (f0649cf2ac4fbbe7). The falsifiable question is unchanged: does the shared global-tightening factor keep compressing long-duration index multiples, or does range trade persist with intact labour and calm aggregate credit? The Oct 20 labour print is now delivered only via unverified BBC summaries (c1fb64abaf970055, 6406ed19b596a94d): headline joblessness easing but vacancies falling on small-firm hiring retrenchment — directionally consistent with soft labour conditions, which weakens the second-round hawkish branch rather than resolving either. The Oct 21 CPI print is the scheduled test that distinguishes an energy-only rise (escalation weakens) from verified second-round effects (escalation strengthens). The quote below both SMAs with a negative MACD histogram remains diagnostic of neither hypothesis. No order is warranted; any future conversion additionally requires fresh quotes, measured CFD costs and decomposed policy expectations.",
        "countercase": "Strongest case against holding: the short-side branch is half-confirmed — the index sits below both SMAs with a negative MACD histogram, HY OAS has widened materially over 20 sessions while the Fed hikes and the BoE leans hawkish — so waiting through the Oct 21 CPI print forgoes trend if discount-rate compression accelerates. Strongest case against a short is equally concrete: Monday's rebound snapshots (QQQ +1.37%, MU +3.07%) cut against the stress narrative, HY near 3.31% remains historically tight, BBB near 1.06% and deeply loose NFCI show no systemic strain, and the committee established that VIX, HY OAS and Friday's heavy QQQ volume are one correlated cluster, not independent confirmations — shorting now risks expressing a single unresolved event. A third objection: today's BBC flow, if corroborated, already weakens the GBP second-round leg, so the shared-factor thesis is eroding and holding cash merely delays acknowledging that. That is precisely why no long is opened either — the softening-labour branch must be confirmed against the ONS release and tomorrow's CPI before any directional file exists. A flat GBP 1,500 demo book forgoes learning, but the mandate's patient-thesis requirement and a catalyst arriving within 24 hours outweigh that; a six-hour-plus hold is an ordinary minimum, never a timer.",
        "invalidation": "Hold converts to a directional file only with specific source-backed resolution: (1) the Oct 21 UK CPI print — confirmed second-round wage/price effects escalate the global-tightening branch; an energy-only rise with soft labour weakens it and, if corroborated by the ONS labour detail behind today's BBC vacancy report (c1fb64abaf970055, 6406ed19b596a94d), narrows the short-side case; (2) HY OAS sustained above ~3.5-3.6% with continued high-volume breadth losses would justify researching a short index file; (3) the 10y below ~4.9% with HY back under ~3.0% and index volume normalising would close the rate-compression line and justify researching a long file; (4) readable OIS/SEP decomposition separating expected policy from term premium. New price levels alone resolve only pricing objections and never trigger trades. Any conversion requires fresh-session quotes, measured spread and a full holding-cost budget; the instrument is a price-only CFD with swap fields as metadata. Uncertified full-filing diligence may justify continued holding even where a directional thesis matures.",
